The Kullback-Leibler Divergence is defined as:
\begin{align*}
  D_{KL}(p \| q_{\thetav}) &= \E_{X \sim p}\left[ \log \left( \frac{p(X)}{q(X|\thetav)} \right)  \right] \\
  &= \underbrace{\E_{X \sim p} \left[ \log(p(X)) \right]}_{(a)} - \underbrace{\E_{X \sim p} \left[ \log(q(X|\thetav)) \right] }_{(b)}
\end{align*}

As we are looking for the set of parameters $\thetav$ that minimizes $D_{KL}(p \| q_{\thetav})$,  we know the following:

\begin{itemize}
  \item (a) does not depend on $\thetav$, and can be considered as a constant.
  \item To minimize $D_{KL}(p \| q_{\thetav})$ is equivalent to maximize (b)
\end{itemize}

Using the definition of the normal distribution:

\begin{align*}
  (b) &=  \E_{X \sim p} \left[ \log(q(X|\thetav)) \right] \\
  &= \E_{X \sim p} \left[\log \left( \frac{1}{\sqrt{\sigma^2 2 \pi }} \right) - \frac{1}{2} \frac{(X-\mu)^2}{\sigma^2}\right]  \\
  &=   \log \left( \frac{1}{\sqrt{\sigma^2 2 \pi }} \right)  -   \E_{X \sim p} \left[ \frac{1}{2} \frac{(X-\mu)^2}{\sigma^2} \right]\\
  &=   -\log  \sqrt{\sigma^2 2 \pi }  -   \underbrace{ \E_{X \sim p} \left[ \frac{1}{2} \frac{X^2 - 2 X \mu + \mu^2}{\sigma^2}  \right]  }_{(c)}
\end{align*}

Solving the component (c) we get:

\begin{align*}
  (c) &= \frac{1}{2 \sigma^2}  \underbrace{\E_{X \sim p} \left[ X^2\right]}_{\var(X) + \E_{X \sim p}[X]^2}
  - \frac{2\mu}{2\sigma^2} \E_{X \sim p} [X]
  + \frac{\mu^2}{2\sigma^2}  \\
  & = \frac{2 \sigma_0^2 + \mu_0^2}{2\sigma^2} - \frac{\mu \mu_0}{\sigma^2} + \frac{\mu^2}{2\sigma^2}
\end{align*}

Plugging (c) into (b), we get the expression that we want to maximize:

$$ (b) = -\log  \sqrt{\sigma^2 2 \pi } - \frac{2 \sigma_0^2 + \mu_0^2}{2\sigma^2} + \frac{\mu \mu_0}{\sigma^2} - \frac{\mu^2}{2\sigma^2} $$

To maximize (b), we differentiate the expression with respect to each parameter. We also need to compute the second derivative to be sure that the point is a maximum.

First, we differentiate with respect to the mean parameter $\mu$:

$$ \frac{\partial (b)}{\partial \mu} =  0 - 0 + \frac{\mu_0}{\sigma^2} - \frac{\mu}{\sigma^2} \overset{!}{=} 0 \longrightarrow \mu_{opt} = \mu_0 $$

This value of $\mu$ is a possible maximum, we check the second derivative:

$$ \frac{\partial^2 (b)}{\partial \mu^2} = - \frac{1}{\sigma^2} < 0 $$

As the second derivative is less than 0 at any point, $\mu_{opt}$ maximizes (b) and  minimizes the Kullback-Leibler divergence accordingly. We now differentiate with respect to the variance parameter $\sigma^2$:

\begin{align*}
  \frac{\partial (b)}{\partial \sigma^2} &= - \frac{1}{2\sigma^2}
      + \frac{2 \sigma_0^2 + \mu_0^2}{2\sigma^4}
      - \frac{\mu \mu_0}{\sigma^4}
      + \frac{\mu^2}{2\sigma^4} \\
  &= - \frac{1}{2\sigma^2} + \frac{2 \sigma_0^2 + \mu_0^2 - 2 \mu \mu_0 + \mu^2}{2\sigma^4} \\
  &= - \frac{1}{2\sigma^2} + \frac{2 \sigma_0^2 + (\mu - \mu_0)^2}{2\sigma^4} \overset{!}{=} 0
  \longrightarrow \sigma_{opt}^2 = 2 \sigma_0^2 + \underbrace{(\mu - \mu_0)^2}_{= 0 \text{ if } \mu = \mu_{opt}}
\end{align*}

This value of $\sigma^2$ is a possible maximum, we check the second derivative:

\begin{align*}
  \frac{\partial^2 (b)}{\partial (\sigma^2)^2} &= \frac{1}{2\sigma^4}- \frac{2 \sigma_0^2 + (\mu - \mu_0)^2}{\sigma^6} \\
  \frac{\partial^2 (b)}{\partial (\sigma^2)^2}\Big|_{\sigma^2 = \sigma_{opt}^2} &= \frac{1}{2 (2 \sigma_0^2 + (\mu - \mu_0)^2)^2} - \frac{1}{(2 \sigma_0^2 + (\mu - \mu_0)^2)^2} < 0
\end{align*}

As the second derivative is less than 0 at the point we are looking, $\sigma_{opt}^2$ maximizes (b) and thus minimizes the Kullback-Leibler Divergence.

